  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Find $x\\in[0,2\\pi]$ for which:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\tan 2x=\\tan x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=0,\\pi,2\\pi$.\n\nUse the period $\\pi$ of tangent.",
          "worked": "Tangent has period $\\pi$, so equal tangent values have arguments differing by an integer multiple of $\\pi$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2x&=x+k\\pi\\\\x&=k\\pi\\\\x&=0,\\pi,2\\pi.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth tangent expressions are defined at these values.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 45
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\cos 3x=\\cos 2x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=0,\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},\\dfrac{4\\pi}{5},\\dfrac{6\\pi}{5},\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5},2\\pi$.\n\nUse the symmetry and period of cosine.",
          "worked": "By the symmetry and period of cosine, either\n$$\\begin{align*}3x&=2x+2k\\pi\\\\x&=2k\\pi\\end{align*}$$\nor\n$$\\begin{align*}3x&=-2x+2k\\pi\\\\5x&=2k\\pi\\\\x&=\\frac{2k\\pi}{5}.\\end{align*}$$\nRestricting to $[0,2\\pi]$ and removing duplicates gives\n$$x=0,\\frac{2\\pi}{5},\\frac{4\\pi}{5},\\frac{6\\pi}{5},\\frac{8\\pi}{5},2\\pi.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\sin 4x=\\sin 3x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{7},\\dfrac{3\\pi}{7},\\dfrac{5\\pi}{7},\\pi,\\dfrac{9\\pi}{7},\\dfrac{11\\pi}{7},\\dfrac{13\\pi}{7},2\\pi$.\n\nUse the symmetry and period of sine.",
          "worked": "By the symmetry and period of sine, either\n$$\\begin{align*}4x&=3x+2k\\pi\\\\x&=2k\\pi\\end{align*}$$\nor\n$$\\begin{align*}4x&=\\pi-3x+2k\\pi\\\\7x&=(2k+1)\\pi\\\\x&=\\frac{(2k+1)\\pi}{7}.\\end{align*}$$\nWithin $[0,2\\pi]$, the first case gives $0,2\\pi$ and the second uses $k=0,1,\\ldots,6$. Hence\n$$x=0,\\frac{\\pi}{7},\\frac{3\\pi}{7},\\frac{5\\pi}{7},\\pi,\\frac{9\\pi}{7},\\frac{11\\pi}{7},\\frac{13\\pi}{7},2\\pi.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q5-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\sin x=\\cos 2x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$.\n\nUse $\\cos 2x=1-2\\sin^2x$ and factorise.",
          "worked": "Using $\\cos 2x=1-2\\sin^2x$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin x&=1-2\\sin^2x\\\\2\\sin^2x+\\sin x-1&=0\\\\(2\\sin x-1)(\\sin x+1)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $\\sin x=\\tfrac12$ or $\\sin x=-1$. The reference angle for $\\tfrac12$ is $\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}$, with sine positive in Quadrants I and II. Therefore\n$$x=\\frac{\\pi}{6},\\frac{5\\pi}{6},\\frac{3\\pi}{2}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
  },
